// automations.memql -- workbench namespace
//
// Workspace lifecycle is driven by the parent RUN's terminal status. When a run
// reaches succeeded / failed / cancelled / abandoned, the workbench workspace is
// released and its filesystem state is torn down. The Go integration owns the
// on-disk teardown; this automation flips the concept's lifecycle bit and emits
// the event the integration listens for.
//
// It was keyed on a Plan until memql#5053. Two things changed with the subject,
// and neither is a rename:
//
//   - `abandoned` joins the terminal set. A run whose node stopped answering is
//     terminal and its directory is orphaned; a Plan had no equivalent status,
//     so the old vocabulary had three values and this one has four. Missing it
//     would leak a workspace per lost node, silently.
//   - The trigger carries a @filter. A run writes a heartbeat at EVERY step
//     boundary, so `graph.node.updated.v1:work:run` fires orders of magnitude
//     more often than the Plan topic did -- and without the filter this
//     automation would execute on every one of them to decide it had nothing to
//     do. The steps still gate on the terminal scalar; the filter is what stops
//     the run from being paid for.

/// When a run transitions to a terminal status (succeeded / failed / cancelled / abandoned),
/// release the associated workbench workspace if one exists and tear down its on-disk directory.
/// terminal (pure logic runIsTerminal) owns the terminal-status vocabulary ONCE (#2370); decide
/// (pure) returns the run's workspaces; apply releases each still-provisioned one and teardown
/// removes the directory tree (even when no concept row exists -- the integration provisions
/// without writing the row), both gated on the terminal scalar.
@trigger(event="graph.node.updated.v1:work:run")
@filter(payload.status != "running" && payload.status != "compiling" && payload.status != "waiting")
automation releaseWorkspaceOnRunTerminal {
  args {
    id any
    status any
  }

  terminal := logic runIsTerminal(status: args.status)
  decide := logic releaseWorkspaceOnRunTerminal(event: event)
  if terminal == true && exists(args.id) {
    teardown := builtin workbenchTeardownDirectory(runId: args.id)
  }
  // memqlmigrate: moved below teardown -- the engine ran this after it, ordering steps by the references it could see rather than by source (memql#5373)
  for item in decide.nodes() {
    if terminal == true && item.status == "provisioned" {
      mutation releaseWorkspace(
        workspaceId: item.id,
        reason: "run_terminal"
      )
    }
  }
}
